  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 455 AM EDT TUE AUG 11 2026\n\nThe most current model data continues to present a pattern aloft \nthat favors the formation of storm clusters - most of which will \nimpinge on the weather for eastern Kentucky through the bulk of \nthe week. Right now, SPC has continued a severe threat into \nThursday for the bulk of our area. Meanwhile, WPC continues to\nhighlight excessive rain threats through Friday - though the \nconcern is higher for earlier in the week. Will continue to \nhighlight this in messaging and social media. Please keep \nmonitoring this developing, likely impactful, weather situation.\n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nActive weather will continue in the long term period, with multiple \nbouts of thunderstorms probable. However, confidence in details is \nstill very elusive.\n\nWe will start the period still under west northwest flow in the mid-\nupper levels, near the southern edge of faster flow, and with \nsubtropical ridging just to our south southwest. A very warm and \nhumid surface air mass will remain in place and give considerable \ninstability. This completes the setup for MCSs to develop where \nthere's enough focus from initiating features. The forecasting \nproblems are that these features are relatively weak in the big \nscheme of things, and also that future convection relies in part on \nthe evolution of difficult to forecast prior convection. Have used a \nbroadbrushed forecast for POP through Friday based on blended \nmodels. \n\nThe GFS and ECMWF both show potential for a weak cold front to slip \nsouth southwest through the JKL forecast area by the weekend as a \nweak low rotates through a northeast CONUS trough and enhances the \nupper level flow over the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley. Should this \ncome to pass, there could be a break in the precip potential at the \nstart of the weekend. Any relief from the muggy air mass would \nprobably be minimal, as the upper troughing quickly departs and \nridging builds into our area from the southwest during the weekend \nand supports hot and humid weather.\n\nModels then show another impulse moving through the faster flow to \nour north and northeast, which suppresses the ridging back to the \nsouthwest early in the new week. This would allow another weak cold \nfront to push in from the north with an increase in the POP in the \nneighborhood of Monday.",
